WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.741 --> 00:00:02.211
- [Narrator] You remember this can.

00:00:02.211 --> 00:00:04.272
The sweet sauce, the soft noodle.

00:00:04.272 --> 00:00:05.750
This was childhood.

00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:06.760
- [Child] Mmmm!

00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:08.069
- [Narrator] But did you know that

00:00:08.069 --> 00:00:09.949
Chef Boyardee was an actual chef?

00:00:09.949 --> 00:00:14.388
He was, his name was Hector,
and he made a mean pasta sauce.

00:00:14.388 --> 00:00:16.770
But it wasn't Hector's sauce
that made Chef Boyardee

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:18.664
the household name that it is today.

00:00:18.664 --> 00:00:21.948
You can thank the U.S. Military for that.

00:00:21.948 --> 00:00:24.777
In the 1930s, Hector
Boiardi opened up an Italian

00:00:24.777 --> 00:00:26.937
restaurant in Ohio and everyone was like,

00:00:26.937 --> 00:00:29.129
- [Man] Oh my god, I love your sauce.

00:00:29.129 --> 00:00:30.767
- [Narrator] So he started selling it,

00:00:30.767 --> 00:00:32.878
and he sold a lot of it.

00:00:32.878 --> 00:00:34.335
A lot, a lot.

00:00:34.335 --> 00:00:37.065
By 1938, he had opened up his own factory

00:00:37.065 --> 00:00:39.308
selling his sauce and pasta can.

00:00:39.308 --> 00:00:41.668
- [Man] Ask for Chef
Boyardee's spaghetti dinner,

00:00:41.668 --> 00:00:44.127
only about 15 cents a serving.

00:00:44.127 --> 00:00:45.957
- [Narrator] Then, World War II happened

00:00:45.957 --> 00:00:48.289
and a phone call was made.

00:00:48.289 --> 00:00:50.718
The U.S. Military called Hector
and said something like.

00:00:50.718 --> 00:00:52.398
- [Man] Help make food for the troops.

00:00:52.398 --> 00:00:53.588
- [Narrator] He already had the canning

00:00:53.588 --> 00:00:56.092
infrastructure so Hector
said, yeah, alright.

00:00:56.092 --> 00:00:57.598
His company shifted from a civilian

00:00:57.598 --> 00:01:00.119
consumer base to a military one.

00:01:00.119 --> 00:01:01.862
Chef Boyardee became the largest supplier

00:01:01.862 --> 00:01:04.720
of rations to U.S. and allied forces.

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:07.452
Production was happening 24/7.

00:01:07.452 --> 00:01:09.913
By the end of the war,
the factory was too large

00:01:09.913 --> 00:01:12.193
and the military demand no longer existed.

00:01:12.193 --> 00:01:13.982
Hector didn't know what to do,

00:01:13.982 --> 00:01:17.161
so he sold it to this large company
called American Home Products

00:01:17.161 --> 00:01:18.102
who were all like,

00:01:18.102 --> 00:01:19.320
- [Man] We got this.

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:21.113
- [Narrator] They quickly
rebranded and distributed

00:01:21.113 --> 00:01:22.941
the can as easy to make at-home meals

00:01:22.941 --> 00:01:26.422
and filled supermarket shelves
across the United States.

00:01:26.422 --> 00:01:28.638
So today when we buy Chef Boyardee,

00:01:28.638 --> 00:01:33.396
we're actually eating rebranded
World War II field rations.

00:01:33.396 --> 00:01:34.229
Yup.

